server: cancel_process_async() holds the async while it cancels it

cancel_process_async() (NtCancelIoFile, NtCancelIoFileEx) cancelled an async
and then used it -- attached the cancel object, moved it to the tracked list
-- without a reference of its own. When no APC can be queued to the async's
thread, async_terminate() completes the async at once: async_set_result()
drops the queue's reference, often the last one (an overlapped read keeps no
wait handle), and the async is destroyed inside cancel_async(). The freed
async was then written to and linked back into process->asyncs; when its
memory came back as a new async, the list turned into a cycle, and the next
walk of it (cancel_terminating_thread_asyncs() at a thread's exit) spun for
ever: the wineserver at 100 % CPU, every client waiting for its reply. The
cancel object was also attached only after the completion that should have
released it, so NtCancelIoFile's wait for it never ended.

On Haiku the APC cannot be queued whenever the thread is not waiting: the
server cannot signal a thread there (ntdll has no Unix thread ID, unix_tid is
-1, so send_thread_signal() always fails). CancelIo() by the reading thread
hits it every time; services.exe and rpcrt4's CancelIoEx from another thread
when the reader is not yet waiting -- about one fresh prefix in 15 (wineboot -i)
left the wineserver spinning.

Found from a core of the spinning wineserver (process->asyncs a cycle of two
freed asyncs), then with AddressSanitizer on an x86-64 Linux wineserver whose
send_thread_signal() fails as on Haiku: tools/wine-tests/cancelio.c gives a
heap-use-after-free in cancel_process_async() at once (freed by
async_terminate() <- fd_cancel_async() <- cancel_async()).

The async is now held across the cancel and gets its cancel object before
it, and the function keeps a count of its own on the cancel object, so a
completion during the loop neither signals nor frees it under us.

--- a/server/async.c
+++ b/server/async.c
@@ -667,6 +667,9 @@
     int count = 0;
 
     if (thread && !(cancel = create_async_cancel( process ))) return 0;
+    /* a count of our own: the cancel object is not signaled and released while we use it, even when the
+     * asyncs complete during their cancel_async() */
+    if (cancel) cancel->count++;
 
     list_init( &tracked );
 
@@ -682,15 +685,21 @@
             (!thread || async->thread == thread) &&
             (!iosb || async->data.iosb == iosb))
         {
-            if (!async->canceled) cancel_async( async );
+            /* cancel_async() may complete the async at once: when no APC can be queued to its thread,
+             * async_set_result() runs from async_terminate() and drops the queue's reference, which may be
+             * the last one. So hold a reference until the async is on the tracked list, and attach the
+             * cancel object first, for that completion to account for it. */
+            grab_object( async );
             if (cancel)
             {
                 assert( !async->async_cancel );
                 async->async_cancel = cancel;
                 cancel->count++;
             }
+            if (!async->canceled) cancel_async( async );
             list_remove( &async->process_entry );
             list_add_tail( &tracked, &async->process_entry );
+            release_object( async );
             count++;
             goto restart;
         }
@@ -703,8 +712,9 @@
     }
     if (cancel)
     {
-        if (!cancel->count) release_object( cancel );
-        else *wait_handle = alloc_handle( process, cancel, SYNCHRONIZE, 0 );
+        /* asyncs still to complete: the caller waits for them */
+        if (cancel->count > 1) *wait_handle = alloc_handle( process, cancel, SYNCHRONIZE, 0 );
+        if (!--cancel->count) release_object( cancel );
     }
     return count;
 }
